WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.195
Lesson one point one.

00:00:02.195 --> 00:00:05.170
What DeFi is, and the risk-first mindset.

00:00:05.170 --> 00:00:06.888
By the end, you'll be able to

00:00:06.888 --> 00:00:09.237
describe the DeFi stack, and explain why

00:00:09.237 --> 00:00:11.241
every yield is payment for a risk,

00:00:11.241 --> 00:00:13.475
whether or not the website mentions it.

00:00:14.775 --> 00:00:16.880
Here's the shift this lesson makes.

00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:21.251
DeFi replaces intermediaries, banks, brokers, exchanges, with

00:00:21.251 --> 00:00:24.705
smart contracts: public programs on a blockchain

00:00:24.705 --> 00:00:27.825
that hold assets and follow fixed rules.

00:00:27.825 --> 00:00:30.150
Nobody's judgment sits in the middle.

00:00:30.150 --> 00:00:32.380
That's exactly what makes it powerful,

00:00:32.380 --> 00:00:34.738
and exactly what makes it dangerous.

00:00:34.738 --> 00:00:37.533
That absence of a middleman means something very

00:00:37.533 --> 00:00:40.270
practical, too: there's no support line to call

00:00:40.270 --> 00:00:43.179
if a transaction does something you didn't intend,

00:00:43.179 --> 00:00:45.418
and no manager who can reverse it.

00:00:45.418 --> 00:00:48.794
Every protection you'd normally get from an institution

00:00:48.794 --> 00:00:51.326
has to come from your own habits instead,

00:00:51.326 --> 00:00:53.738
which is exactly why this module exists.

00:00:55.038 --> 00:00:56.328
Here's the plan.

00:00:56.328 --> 00:00:59.993
First, the DeFi stack, six layers, bottom to top.

00:00:59.993 --> 00:01:02.733
Second, three properties that make it powerful

00:01:02.733 --> 00:01:04.898
and dangerous, at the same time.

00:01:04.898 --> 00:01:07.258
Third, the risk-first rule: name what you

00:01:07.258 --> 00:01:09.693
could lose before you compare returns.

00:01:09.693 --> 00:01:13.610
And finally, a real worked example, a pool advertising

00:01:13.610 --> 00:01:16.743
12%, and the five questions it should raise.

00:01:18.043 --> 00:01:19.128
The DeFi stack.

00:01:20.428 --> 00:01:23.513
Here's the stack, simplified to its core idea.

00:01:23.513 --> 00:01:25.788
The chain settles every transaction.

00:01:25.788 --> 00:01:28.152
Your wallet holds the keys and signs.

00:01:28.152 --> 00:01:30.494
The app is the smart contract itself,

00:01:30.494 --> 00:01:33.197
doing the swapping, lending or staking.

00:01:33.197 --> 00:01:34.843
And your position is what you

00:01:34.843 --> 00:01:36.953
actually hold, and what it risks.

00:01:36.953 --> 00:01:38.565
Every yield you'll ever see pays

00:01:38.565 --> 00:01:39.933
for something in this chain.

00:01:41.234 --> 00:01:43.074
In full, it's six layers.

00:01:43.074 --> 00:01:46.199
The blockchain records every balance and transaction.

00:01:46.199 --> 00:01:49.311
Smart contracts are the protocol logic itself,

00:01:49.311 --> 00:01:51.929
a lending market, an exchange pool.

00:01:51.929 --> 00:01:54.784
Tokens are the assets those contracts move.

00:01:54.784 --> 00:01:57.601
Wallets are where you hold keys and sign actions.

00:01:57.601 --> 00:02:00.809
Interfaces are websites that build transactions for

00:02:00.809 --> 00:02:03.391
you, a convenience, not the protocol.

00:02:03.391 --> 00:02:06.045
And data layers, explorers and dashboards,

00:02:06.045 --> 00:02:08.391
let you verify what actually happened.

00:02:09.691 --> 00:02:13.549
Here's why memorising six layers actually matters: each one

00:02:13.549 --> 00:02:16.936
is also a place something specific can go wrong.

00:02:16.936 --> 00:02:20.231
The chain can reorg, go down, or get congested.

00:02:20.231 --> 00:02:22.711
Contracts can contain bugs, or be upgraded

00:02:22.711 --> 00:02:24.741
in ways that don't favour you.

00:02:24.741 --> 00:02:26.671
Tokens can lose their peg.

00:02:26.671 --> 00:02:29.126
Wallets can be compromised or phished.

00:02:29.126 --> 00:02:31.026
Interfaces can be spoofed.

00:02:31.026 --> 00:02:34.326
And data layers can simply be misread, or wrong.

00:02:34.326 --> 00:02:36.871
Six layers, six distinct failure modes.

00:02:38.171 --> 00:02:41.561
One layer deserves special attention: the interface.

00:02:41.561 --> 00:02:43.766
A website is not the protocol.

00:02:43.766 --> 00:02:45.438
It's a convenience that builds

00:02:45.438 --> 00:02:47.626
transactions for you, nothing more.

00:02:47.626 --> 00:02:51.457
Interfaces can be faked, compromised, or simply wrong, while

00:02:51.457 --> 00:02:55.036
the underlying contracts keep working exactly as written.

00:02:56.336 --> 00:02:57.861
Powerful and dangerous.

00:02:59.161 --> 00:03:01.345
Three properties define DeFi, and

00:03:01.345 --> 00:03:03.186
each one cuts both ways.

00:03:03.186 --> 00:03:06.719
Self-custody means nobody can freeze your funds; it also

00:03:06.719 --> 00:03:10.366
means nobody can recover them if something goes wrong.

00:03:10.366 --> 00:03:14.170
Composability means protocols plug into each other, making

00:03:14.170 --> 00:03:17.265
powerful products; it also means a position can

00:03:17.265 --> 00:03:20.596
fail because of something it merely depends on.

00:03:20.596 --> 00:03:24.212
And transparency means you can verify everything on-chain;

00:03:24.212 --> 00:03:26.296
it doesn't mean any of it is safe.

00:03:27.596 --> 00:03:30.060
You've already met self-custody in

00:03:30.060 --> 00:03:32.401
practice, back in Lesson 0.4.

00:03:32.401 --> 00:03:34.852
An exchange can freeze a withdrawal; a

00:03:34.852 --> 00:03:37.428
wallet only you control cannot be frozen

00:03:37.428 --> 00:03:39.676
by anyone else, for any reason.

00:03:39.676 --> 00:03:42.503
DeFi runs entirely on that second kind

00:03:42.503 --> 00:03:45.331
of custody, by design, not by accident.

00:03:46.631 --> 00:03:50.451
Here's roughly how a composability failure actually cascades,

00:03:50.451 --> 00:03:52.846
so it isn't just an abstract word.

00:03:52.846 --> 00:03:57.476
A price oracle reports a manipulated, momentarily wrong price.

00:03:57.476 --> 00:03:59.863
A lending contract, built to trust that

00:03:59.863 --> 00:04:02.371
oracle completely, acts on it anyway.

00:04:02.371 --> 00:04:05.426
Positions get liquidated that never should have been.

00:04:05.426 --> 00:04:08.476
And the losses land on real depositors, who did

00:04:08.476 --> 00:04:11.826
nothing wrong except depend on that same oracle.

00:04:11.826 --> 00:04:15.696
Lesson 5.3 covers oracle manipulation properly.

00:04:16.996 --> 00:04:19.571
This isn't a hypothetical pattern, either.

00:04:19.571 --> 00:04:25.413
Oracle manipulation causing incorrect liquidations is a well-documented category

00:04:25.413 --> 00:04:28.886
of real DeFi exploits, not a rare edge case.

00:04:28.886 --> 00:04:33.493
It's precisely why serious protocols spend so much engineering

00:04:33.493 --> 00:04:37.222
effort on oracle design, and why Lesson 5.3 treats

00:04:37.222 --> 00:04:39.196
it as a subject on its own.

00:04:40.496 --> 00:04:43.456
Here's composability, made concrete, purely as

00:04:43.456 --> 00:04:46.401
an illustration of how dependencies stack.

00:04:46.401 --> 00:04:48.425
Simply holding a token depends on

00:04:48.425 --> 00:04:50.391
one thing: the chain itself.

00:04:50.391 --> 00:04:53.150
A basic lending deposit depends on three:

00:04:53.150 --> 00:04:55.843
the chain, the lending contract, and the

00:04:55.843 --> 00:04:57.851
price oracle it relies on.

00:04:57.851 --> 00:05:01.454
And a leveraged position across multiple protocols can

00:05:01.454 --> 00:05:04.270
depend on five or more: the chain, several

00:05:04.270 --> 00:05:07.256
contracts, an oracle, and often a bridge.

00:05:07.256 --> 00:05:09.512
More layers, more yield, usually more

00:05:09.512 --> 00:05:11.471
places for something to go wrong.

00:05:12.771 --> 00:05:15.508
That's the real cost of composability: every

00:05:15.508 --> 00:05:17.455
extra layer you depend on is an

00:05:17.455 --> 00:05:19.462
extra way to lose money that has

00:05:19.462 --> 00:05:22.074
nothing to do with your own decisions.

00:05:22.074 --> 00:05:24.208
Transparency lets you see all of it,

00:05:24.208 --> 00:05:26.168
but seeing a risk and avoiding it

00:05:26.168 --> 00:05:27.898
are two very different things.

00:05:29.198 --> 00:05:30.383
The risk-first rule.

00:05:31.683 --> 00:05:34.108
Here's the whole rule, in one sentence.

00:05:34.108 --> 00:05:36.076
Before comparing returns, list what

00:05:36.076 --> 00:05:37.798
could make you lose money.

00:05:37.798 --> 00:05:39.995
A higher yield means you're being paid

00:05:39.995 --> 00:05:41.967
to carry more risk, whether or not

00:05:41.967 --> 00:05:43.901
the website ever mentions it.

00:05:43.901 --> 00:05:45.405
If you can't name the risk,

00:05:45.405 --> 00:05:47.661
you haven't actually evaluated the return.

00:05:48.960 --> 00:05:51.750
Try it on a familiar shape of comparison.

00:05:51.750 --> 00:05:55.098
An ordinary savings product paying a modest rate

00:05:55.098 --> 00:05:58.172
is boring precisely because its risk is well

00:05:58.172 --> 00:06:01.205
understood and, in many places, insured.

00:06:01.205 --> 00:06:03.981
A DeFi pool paying several times that on the

00:06:03.981 --> 00:06:07.127
same kind of dollar carries no such insurance, and

00:06:07.127 --> 00:06:11.005
probably several dependencies from the stack you just saw.

00:06:11.005 --> 00:06:12.890
The extra yield isn't free.

00:06:12.890 --> 00:06:15.620
It's the price of everything this lesson just described.

00:06:16.920 --> 00:06:20.225
You'll apply this exact rule constantly from here on.

00:06:20.225 --> 00:06:23.965
Stablecoin risk gets its own lesson, 1.5.

00:06:23.965 --> 00:06:27.335
Oracle risk gets its own lesson, 5.3.

00:06:27.335 --> 00:06:31.560
Protocol due diligence gets an entire lesson, 6.1.

00:06:31.560 --> 00:06:34.311
This single mental model, name the risk before

00:06:34.311 --> 00:06:36.476
the return, is what most of the rest

00:06:36.476 --> 00:06:38.525
of this program actually builds on.

00:06:39.825 --> 00:06:40.890
A worked example.

00:06:42.190 --> 00:06:43.875
Here's the worked example.

00:06:43.875 --> 00:06:46.493
A pool advertises 12% APY on

00:06:46.493 --> 00:06:50.405
a stablecoin, purely as an illustration.

00:06:50.405 --> 00:06:52.899
Before depositing, ask five things.

00:06:52.899 --> 00:06:56.137
Where does the 12% actually come from: borrowers

00:06:56.137 --> 00:06:59.794
paying interest, trading fees, or a reward token?

00:06:59.794 --> 00:07:02.672
Which stablecoin is it, and what actually

00:07:02.672 --> 00:07:05.559
backs it, the subject of Lesson 1.5?

00:07:05.559 --> 00:07:07.659
Which contracts hold the money, and who

00:07:07.659 --> 00:07:09.654
has the power to upgrade them?

00:07:09.654 --> 00:07:10.886
Is there a bridge or

00:07:10.886 --> 00:07:13.184
a price oracle quietly involved?

00:07:13.184 --> 00:07:14.687
And how do you withdraw, and

00:07:14.687 --> 00:07:16.449
could withdrawals ever be blocked?

00:07:17.749 --> 00:07:20.396
Here's the difference between an answer that actually

00:07:20.396 --> 00:07:22.754
tells you something, and one that doesn't.

00:07:22.754 --> 00:07:26.105
A real answer sounds specific: borrowers pay a base

00:07:26.105 --> 00:07:29.198
rate, the rest is a temporary reward token that

00:07:29.198 --> 00:07:31.754
will taper off on a known schedule.

00:07:31.754 --> 00:07:34.664
A red flag sounds like reassurance instead of

00:07:34.664 --> 00:07:38.569
information: just trust the protocol, it's always worked.

00:07:38.569 --> 00:07:41.303
Vague and permanent-sounding is exactly the

00:07:41.303 --> 00:07:42.857
shape a bad answer takes.

00:07:44.157 --> 00:07:46.842
Notice what this exercise actually is.

00:07:46.842 --> 00:07:49.047
It isn't cynicism about DeFi.

00:07:49.047 --> 00:07:51.506
It's the same due diligence a bank loan

00:07:51.506 --> 00:07:54.458
officer runs before approving a rate, just done

00:07:54.458 --> 00:07:57.101
by you, because here, nobody else is going

00:07:57.101 --> 00:07:58.577
to do it on your behalf.

00:07:59.877 --> 00:08:01.057
Checklist and quiz.

00:08:02.357 --> 00:08:04.252
Here's this lesson's checklist.

00:08:04.252 --> 00:08:05.907
You can name the six layers of

00:08:05.907 --> 00:08:08.342
the DeFi stack, without looking them up.

00:08:08.342 --> 00:08:10.698
You can explain why self-custody cuts

00:08:10.698 --> 00:08:12.672
both ways, in one sentence.

00:08:12.672 --> 00:08:15.179
And before any yield, you ask yourself: what

00:08:15.179 --> 00:08:17.017
am I actually being paid to risk?

00:08:18.317 --> 00:08:19.527
Question one.

00:08:19.527 --> 00:08:21.792
Why can composability increase risk?

00:08:26.212 --> 00:08:29.487
The answer: a position inherits the failure risk

00:08:29.487 --> 00:08:32.629
of every protocol, asset, oracle and bridge it

00:08:32.629 --> 00:08:34.567
depends on, not just its own.

00:08:35.867 --> 00:08:37.112
Question two.

00:08:37.112 --> 00:08:39.517
Is a website the same thing as the protocol?

00:08:43.937 --> 00:08:47.817
The answer: no. The interface only builds transactions.

00:08:47.817 --> 00:08:50.546
The contracts are the actual protocol, and

00:08:50.546 --> 00:08:53.022
interfaces can be faked or compromised.

00:08:54.322 --> 00:08:55.592
Question three.

00:08:55.592 --> 00:08:58.267
What's the risk-first mindset, in one sentence?

00:09:02.687 --> 00:09:05.147
The answer: identify what could make you

00:09:05.147 --> 00:09:07.786
lose money, before you ever compare returns.

00:09:09.086 --> 00:09:10.341
Question four.

00:09:10.341 --> 00:09:11.498
A pool offers a much

00:09:11.498 --> 00:09:13.670
higher yield than everywhere else.

00:09:13.670 --> 00:09:15.295
What should that immediately tell you?

00:09:19.715 --> 00:09:22.352
The answer: that you're being paid to carry more

00:09:22.352 --> 00:09:24.935
risk somewhere, whether or not it's obvious yet.

00:09:26.235 --> 00:09:27.550
Question five.

00:09:27.550 --> 00:09:30.387
What does self-custody take away, in exchange for

00:09:30.387 --> 00:09:32.600
nobody being able to freeze your funds?

00:09:37.020 --> 00:09:39.267
The answer: nobody can recover them for

00:09:39.267 --> 00:09:41.290
you either, if something goes wrong.

00:09:42.590 --> 00:09:45.400
Here's the whole lesson, recapped as one loop.

00:09:45.400 --> 00:09:48.305
Know the stack, chain, wallet, app, position.

00:09:48.305 --> 00:09:51.000
Remember the interface isn't the protocol.

00:09:51.000 --> 00:09:53.835
Name what composability adds you depend on.

00:09:53.835 --> 00:09:57.800
Verify, don't just trust, because transparency isn't safety.

00:09:57.800 --> 00:09:59.913
And always ask what you're being paid

00:09:59.913 --> 00:10:01.970
to risk, before comparing any return.

00:10:03.270 --> 00:10:06.263
This is education, not financial advice, and

00:10:06.263 --> 00:10:08.791
every number in this lesson, the 12%,

00:10:08.791 --> 00:10:11.685
the dependency counts, is illustrative.

00:10:11.685 --> 00:10:14.459
No yield in DeFi is guaranteed, and none of

00:10:14.459 --> 00:10:17.675
it is risk-free; it's always payment for something.

00:10:18.975 --> 00:10:22.030
That's what DeFi is, and the risk-first mindset:

00:10:22.030 --> 00:10:25.272
the six-layer stack, three properties that cut both

00:10:25.272 --> 00:10:28.440
ways, and naming your risk before your return.

00:10:28.440 --> 00:10:30.194
Next, Lesson one point two:

00:10:30.194 --> 00:10:32.387
how a transaction actually happens.
